narrative:
## Layered Validity Constraints

Age has multiple rules: must be non-negative, must be realistic (no 200-year-olds claiming benefits), and different thresholds unlock different capabilities. Multiple invariants enforce all rules simultaneously:

```xiom
type Age = {
  years: Int;
  invariant: years >= 0;
  invariant: years <= 150;
}

fn Age.is_adult(self: &Self) -> Bool {
  return self.years >= 18;
}

fn Age.next_birthday(self: &Self) -> Age {
  let new_age = self.years + 1;
  if new_age > 150 { return self; }
  return Age{ years: new_age };
}
```

Each invariant stands alone but works together. The type system checks all of them every time an `Age` is created or mutated. Functions that operate on `Age` can assume both invariants hold:

```xiom
fn Age.can_drive(self: &Self) -> Bool {
  return self.years >= 16;
}

fn Age.can_vote(self: &Self) -> Bool {
  return self.years >= 18;
}
```

These methods are safe because the invariants guarantee `years` is always in range. No need to check `years >= 0` before comparing — the type itself made that promise.

